5417] [17592186059897 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059897 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "21fdb5f1-db87-5c60-b2e3-449b3c8d22a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060316 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186058518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060316 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060316 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e2/e2ba81c227223d872727ceec1e6b097b69d3f4b47cd9f7190ab984c2124c1bf5.png)\n\nFor the triangle above, find $m∠A$ in degrees rounded to two decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060316 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a1ec4ee-60c9-5336-ad34-95ed5dc973af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060316 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060493 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060493 #edb/ref 1061 11024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060493 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4e5717a6-68f0-5158-8484-d4001a77e16f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060493 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060494 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060494 #edb/ref 1061 11025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060494 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb62fd93-0b20-52e8-bd24-8239ba33c0bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060494 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060495 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060495 #edb/ref 1061 11028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060495 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a5b6a9a3-0ce2-5918-bbe5-1f4d666fd6ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060495 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060496 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060496 #edb/ref 1061 11031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060496 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4158324f-f5e3-5d83-8e5e-ae54e6256c2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060496 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060803 #edb/ref 1061 461992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060803 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c0891e57-fce7-50ae-a891-40c8707b98aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060803 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060863 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now derive the law of cosines in the following form:\n\n$$\na^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\\cos A\n$$\n\nConsider $\\triangle ABC$ with sides of lengths $a,b,$ and $c,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/7c/7cc2a0cbb6a02af829e070fc54cfdbe616683f86527b119a530053a34048e699.png)\n\nLet's now add the height $\\overline{CH}$ and assume it has length $h,$ as shown.\n\n![](images/d4/d4e80b2b54283ea353b687d959e9d8fc6c9303e85081c0a46b48703ee3dae7d5.png)\n\nThe remainder of this proof consists of two steps:\n\n- **Step 1**: Write down expressions for $\\cos A$ and $\\sin A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Apply the Pythagorean theorem to $\\triangle BHC,$ and simplify.\n\nUsing elementary trigonometry, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos A = \\dfrac{AH}{b}, \\qquad \\sin A = \\dfrac{h}{b},\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\nAH =  b\\cos A, \\qquad h = b\\sin A.\n$$\n\nApplying the Pythagorean theorem to $\\triangle BHC$ and using our expression for $h,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{h}^{2} \\\\ {a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the length of the side $\\overline{AB}.$ Since $\\overline{AB}$ consists of two segments, $\\overline{AH}$ and $\\overline{BH},$ we have\n\n$$\nc = AH + BH\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad BH = c - AH,\n$$\n\nand using our expression for $AH,$ we have\n\n$$\nBH = c - b\\cos A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression for $a^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & =(BH)^{2}+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & =(c-b\\operatorname{cos}⁡A)^{2}+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & ={c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A+{b}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡A+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A \\\\  & ={b}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡A+{b}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡A+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A \\\\  & ={b}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡A+{sin}^{2}⁡A)+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that $\\cos^2A + \\sin^2 A = 1$ for any angle $A$ (this is the Pythagorean theorem in trigonometric form). Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc\\cos A\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nWe can use a similar argument to prove the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2} &={a}^{2}+{c}^{2}-2ac\\operatorname{cos}⁡B \\\\ {c}^{2} &={a}^{2}+{b}^{2}-2ab\\operatorname{cos}⁡C\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060863 #edb/ref 1065 13281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060863 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6ee27fb2-d873-53a9-b674-446b546b19ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060863 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Law of Cosines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060882 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a general triangle where\n\n- side $a$ is *opposite* angle $\\angle A,$\n\n- side $b$ is *opposite* angle $\\angle B,$ and\n\n- side $c$ is *opposite* angle $\\angle C,$\n\nas follows:\n\n![](images/8f/8f3d3bf4824508ef152e8d8d567672d7d61fb36e0ffb533d97be7294e9198956.png)\n\nThe **law of cosines** states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & ={b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A \\\\ {b}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{c}^{2}-2ac\\operatorname{cos}⁡B \\\\ {c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2}-2ab\\operatorname{cos}⁡C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use this to calculate the length of one side when we know two sides and one angle.\n\nIf we look at the law of cosines we see that it's quite similar to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\na^2+b^2=c^2\n$$\n\nBut remember that the Pythagorean theorem can *only* be used for a *right-angled triangle*!\n\n![](images/db/db9ed4aac9ca46ffc95d393d863cb09e9ba874caf0008410937f961fdf6c7ab6.png)\n\nThe law of cosines is more general and can be used on *any* triangle.\n\n**Note:** To remember the law of cosines, it's helpful to notice that the equations always follow the same pattern:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{side opposite}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{the angle}\\,\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}\\,=\\,{\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{adjacent}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{side}\\,\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}\\,+\\,{\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{adjacent}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{other side}\\,\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}\\,-\\,2\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{adjacent}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{side}\\,\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}\\,\\text{adjacent}\\, \\\\ \\,\\text{other side}\\,\\end{pmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\text{angle}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll show how this formula can be derived at the end of the lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060882 #edb/ref 1065 2464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060882 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bdb04bba-3835-534a-8187-8b828854f81e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060882 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060884 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's look at our general triangle again.\n\n![](images/8f/8f3d3bf4824508ef152e8d8d567672d7d61fb36e0ffb533d97be7294e9198956.png)\n\nWhen we know the lengths of three sides of a triangle, we rearrange the law of cosines to find an angle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{2} & ={b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A \\\\ 2bc\\operatorname{cos}⁡A & ={b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{a}^{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡A & =\\frac{{b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{a}^{2}}{2bc}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for all the angles, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡A & =\\frac{{b}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{a}^{2}}{2bc}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡B & =\\frac{{a}^{2}+{c}^{2}-{b}^{2}}{2ac}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡C & =\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}-{c}^{2}}{2ab}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's helpful to notice that the equations always follow the same pattern:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\text{angle})=\\frac{{\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{adjacent} \\\\ \\text{side}\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}+{\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{adjacent} \\\\ \\text{other side}\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}-{\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{side opposite} \\\\ \\text{the angle}\\end{pmatrix}}^{2}}{2\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{adjacent} \\\\ \\text{side}\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}\\text{adjacent} \\\\ \\text{other side}\\end{pmatrix}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060884 #edb/ref 1065 2467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060884 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0bfa5e32-194d-54b1-adf5-f72b45e48fb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060884 #edb/ref 1067 "Using the Law of Cosines to Calculate an Unknown Angle" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
